1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,268
A developer writes in the team channel:
"Everything is green and I still cannot

2
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:14,197
merge." Three people answer with three
guesses. One says to push an empty commit.

3
00:00:14,237 --> 00:00:17,301
One says to ask an
administrator. One says to wait.

4
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:20,527
Which of the three would you
follow? Say it out loud.

5
00:00:25,367 --> 00:00:30,009
Whichever you chose, notice what's missing.
None of them asked the two questions

6
00:00:30,049 --> 00:00:34,926
that end the discussion in five minutes:
which rules apply to this branch, and which

7
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:39,374
Git fact does each rule look at. In this
video you learn where the rules are

8
00:00:39,414 --> 00:00:43,998
visible, how to ask Git the questions the
rules ask, and how to defend a set of

9
00:00:44,038 --> 00:00:48,505
rules for a branch that deploys to
production. Keep that grey button in mind.

10
00:00:48,545 --> 00:00:50,437
It gets its answer in nine steps.

11
00:00:52,277 --> 00:00:56,185
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:56,225 --> 00:01:01,622
In the last videos you learned what a rule
is: a check on a ref update, evaluated by

13
00:01:01,662 --> 00:01:07,059
GitHub and not by Git. A ref is a name, such
as a branch or a tag, that holds the ID

14
00:01:07,099 --> 00:01:07,836
of a commit.

15
00:01:08,176 --> 00:01:13,300
A ruleset is a named list of rules with a
target, an enforcement status and a bypass

16
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:18,341
list. You know that rulesets layer, that
every applicable rule applies, and that a

17
00:01:18,381 --> 00:01:23,136
classic branch protection rule, the older
mechanism, can sit beside a ruleset.

18
00:01:23,476 --> 00:01:28,112
This video turns that knowledge into
a working procedure, in three parts.

19
00:01:28,152 --> 00:01:33,427
First, how you see the rules: the rules
page, the gh ruleset commands, and the REST

20
00:01:33,467 --> 00:01:38,679
API. Second, the nine-step procedure of
section 18.17 for a blocked merge, and its

21
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:44,059
mirror case, "why could they merge?". A pull
request is GitHub's proposal to merge a

22
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:49,439
head branch into a base branch. Third, a
worked design: one ruleset for a production

23
00:01:49,479 --> 00:01:53,474
branch, with the reason for each
rule and what each rule costs.

24
00:01:53,814 --> 00:01:59,153
The local demonstration replays the first
half of Lab 23.3 in the lab sandbox.

25
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:03,636
The second half is a walkthrough
on your own practice repository.

26
00:02:05,476 --> 00:02:07,054
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:02:07,394 --> 00:02:08,806
After this video you can:

28
00:02:09,146 --> 00:02:14,306
find every rule that applies to a branch
from the /rules page and from the command

29
00:02:14,346 --> 00:02:14,623
line;

30
00:02:14,963 --> 00:02:20,398
explain why gh ruleset is read-only and
how rulesets are changed through the API;

31
00:02:20,738 --> 00:02:24,624
follow the procedure for "everything
is green and I still cannot merge";

32
00:02:24,964 --> 00:02:28,186
diagnose three blocked merges
on the practice repository;

33
00:02:28,526 --> 00:02:32,511
defend each rule of the worked
production-branch design and name its cost.

34
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:38,867
Start with why. A blocked merge is a
refusal by GitHub to update a ref.

35
00:02:38,907 --> 00:02:44,192
The refusal has a cause, the cause is one
specific rule, and that rule looks at one

36
00:02:44,232 --> 00:02:48,748
specific thing. If you guess, you
change things that weren't the cause.

37
00:02:48,788 --> 00:02:54,009
And on a branch where a new push dismisses
earlier approvals as stale, every extra

38
00:02:54,049 --> 00:02:59,206
push costs you a review. So the order is
evidence first: which rules apply, which

39
00:02:59,246 --> 00:03:00,810
one says no, and to what.

40
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:05,698
Seeing the rules. The textbook quotes the
documentation: "Anyone with read access to

41
00:03:05,738 --> 00:03:10,067
the repository can view the active rulesets."
There are three documented places.

42
00:03:10,107 --> 00:03:13,726
The first is the Rulesets page that
you reach from the branch list.

43
00:03:13,766 --> 00:03:18,041
The second is the merge box of a pull
request, the panel with the merge button,

44
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:22,519
which shows rules, in the words of the
documentation, "if there are rules blocking

45
00:03:22,559 --> 00:03:27,052
the merging of a pull request". The third
is the address of the repository with the

46
00:03:27,092 --> 00:03:28,035
slug /rules added.

47
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:34,172
Quick quiz, from the last video. Are classic
branch protection rules on that page

48
00:03:34,212 --> 00:03:38,567
too? A, yes, for anyone with
read access. B, no. Your answer?

49
00:03:43,407 --> 00:03:46,630
B. Classic branch protection
rules aren't on that page.

50
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:51,495
They're under the repository settings,
Branches, and only administrators see them.

51
00:03:51,535 --> 00:03:56,479
Keep that sentence in mind, because it
explains a whole class of "I cannot see why".

52
00:03:56,819 --> 00:04:02,481
The command line. The GitHub CLI is a
client for GitHub's API, the interface that

53
00:04:02,521 --> 00:04:06,282
programs use to talk to
GitHub, and its command is gh.

54
00:04:06,322 --> 00:04:10,295
It has a gh ruleset family,
and that family is read-only.

55
00:04:10,335 --> 00:04:15,715
Its own help says: "These commands allow
you to view information about them."

56
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:20,502
In version 2.88.1 there are three
subcommands: list, view and check.

57
00:04:20,842 --> 00:04:25,923
All of these are 🟢 SAFE: they change
nothing. The one to remember is gh ruleset

58
00:04:25,963 --> 00:04:31,109
check. You give it a branch name, the
branch doesn't need to exist, and it lists

59
00:04:31,149 --> 00:04:36,230
every rule that would apply. That's the
answer to the silent-pattern problem of

60
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:41,222
section 18.3: you ask which rules apply
to a name before you trust a pattern.

61
00:04:41,562 --> 00:04:46,922
Changing rules. Creating and changing a
ruleset goes through the REST API, with gh

62
00:04:46,962 --> 00:04:51,400
api. The ruleset travels as JSON,
a text format for structured data.

63
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:56,933
The textbook lists the documented endpoints,
the addresses of the API, and says that

64
00:04:56,973 --> 00:04:59,633
none of them was executed by the authors.

65
00:04:59,973 --> 00:05:04,650
The read calls are 🟢 SAFE. Every call
with POST, PUT or DELETE is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

66
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:09,669
Here are the five answers for the three of
them. What they change: a POST creates a

67
00:05:09,709 --> 00:05:13,781
ruleset, and if it's Active it binds
everyone at once, you included.

68
00:05:13,821 --> 00:05:18,801
A PUT replaces the settings of a ruleset.
A DELETE removes the protection for every

69
00:05:18,841 --> 00:05:21,099
ref the ruleset targeted, immediately.

70
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:26,507
What they can destroy: the POST destroys
nothing, and carries the label because, as

71
00:05:26,547 --> 00:05:31,678
Chapter 15 says, the call does whatever the
endpoint and the JSON say, with all your

72
00:05:31,718 --> 00:05:36,848
permissions. The PUT destroys the previous
settings, which exist nowhere else unless

73
00:05:36,888 --> 00:05:41,157
you saved the JSON, because ruleset
history exists only on Enterprise.

74
00:05:41,197 --> 00:05:46,266
The DELETE destroys nothing directly. It
makes force pushes and deletions possible.

75
00:05:46,606 --> 00:05:51,215
How to preview: create with enforcement
disabled and run gh ruleset check.

76
00:05:51,255 --> 00:05:55,740
Before a PUT or a DELETE, read the
ruleset with a GET and keep the JSON.

77
00:05:55,780 --> 00:06:00,892
How to recover: set enforcement to disabled,
PUT the saved JSON back, or re-create

78
00:06:00,932 --> 00:06:06,170
from the saved JSON. When it's appropriate:
for rules kept as reviewed JSON, and for

79
00:06:06,210 --> 00:06:11,386
a deletion, in a planned migration or a
declared emergency, by a named person, with

80
00:06:11,426 --> 00:06:14,402
the reason written where
the audit will find it.

81
00:06:14,742 --> 00:06:19,296
Two details about the read endpoints.
"Get rules for a branch" returns, in the

82
00:06:19,336 --> 00:06:24,125
documentation's words, "all active rules
that apply to the specified branch", from

83
00:06:24,165 --> 00:06:28,719
every level, and it leaves out rulesets
that are disabled or in evaluate mode.

84
00:06:28,759 --> 00:06:33,431
And the bypass_actors field is returned
only to callers with write access to the

85
00:06:33,471 --> 00:06:33,902
ruleset.

86
00:06:34,242 --> 00:06:38,332
Rulesets can be exported and imported
as JSON in the web interface.

87
00:06:38,372 --> 00:06:43,018
Keeping that JSON in a repository gives
you review and history for the rules

88
00:06:43,058 --> 00:06:48,073
themselves. The bypass list is excluded
from the Enterprise history export, so you

89
00:06:48,113 --> 00:06:49,368
record it separately.

90
00:06:49,708 --> 00:06:55,058
Rule Insights lists ref updates that passed,
failed or bypassed rulesets, and the

91
00:06:55,098 --> 00:07:00,315
rule-suites endpoint returns the same
data. It's your evidence for the question

92
00:07:00,355 --> 00:07:05,638
"would we know if someone skipped the
rules", with one exception: exempt actors,

93
00:07:05,678 --> 00:07:07,901
from section 18.5, leave no entry.

94
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:14,012
In one sentence, in the textbook's
words: work from the outside in.

95
00:07:14,052 --> 00:07:19,418
What the merge box says, which rules apply
to the base branch from every layer, then

96
00:07:19,458 --> 00:07:21,477
one local Git question per rule.

97
00:07:21,817 --> 00:07:26,531
Think of it as two lanes. In one lane you
ask GitHub about platform state: reviews,

98
00:07:26,571 --> 00:07:29,795
checks, conversations, the
allowed merge methods, bypass.

99
00:07:29,835 --> 00:07:34,606
In the other lane you ask Git about facts
of the commit graph: is the branch behind,

100
00:07:34,646 --> 00:07:39,074
does the test merge conflict, are there
merge commits, are the commits signed.

101
00:07:39,114 --> 00:07:43,484
Many rules are a platform decision about
a Git fact, and the Git fact you can

102
00:07:43,524 --> 00:07:45,661
compute yourself, offline, in a clone.

103
00:07:46,001 --> 00:07:50,385
The model breaks in one place, and you
should say so when you explain it.

104
00:07:50,425 --> 00:07:55,234
Git can tell you whether a fact holds.
Git can't tell you whether a rule exists.

105
00:07:55,274 --> 00:08:00,144
A clone contains no rulesets. So the Git
lane never replaces the first two steps.

106
00:08:00,484 --> 00:08:05,709
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
two columns, "ask GitHub" and "ask Git".

107
00:08:05,749 --> 00:08:10,658
Sort these four into them: reviews,
conflicts, required checks, merge commits.

108
00:08:10,698 --> 00:08:12,244
Say your answer out loud.

109
00:08:17,084 --> 00:08:20,890
Reviews and required checks go under
GitHub: they're platform state.

110
00:08:20,930 --> 00:08:25,640
Conflicts and merge commits go under Git:
they're facts of the commit graph, and you

111
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:29,599
can compute them offline, in a clone,
your own copy of the repository.

112
00:08:29,939 --> 00:08:31,268
Here are the nine steps.

113
00:08:31,608 --> 00:08:36,783
One: read the merge box and the CLI's
view. gh pr view with the JSON fields

114
00:08:36,823 --> 00:08:42,415
mergeable, mergeStateStatus and reviewDecision,
and gh pr checks with --required.

115
00:08:42,455 --> 00:08:48,186
mergeable is MERGEABLE, CONFLICTING or
UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN means not computed yet:

116
00:08:48,226 --> 00:08:53,540
ask again. mergeStateStatus has values
such as BEHIND, BLOCKED, DIRTY, DRAFT,

117
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:57,295
UNSTABLE and CLEAN. BLOCKED
says that a rule is unmet.

118
00:08:57,335 --> 00:09:01,050
It doesn't say which one.
The remaining steps find it.

119
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:07,056
Two: list every rule on the base branch. gh
ruleset check with the base branch name,

120
00:09:07,096 --> 00:09:12,491
or the /rules page, then the classic rule,
then remember the organization level.

121
00:09:12,831 --> 00:09:18,909
Three: conflicts. Fetch, then git
merge-tree --write-tree --name-only.

122
00:09:18,949 --> 00:09:21,531
Exit status 1 names the files.

123
00:09:21,871 --> 00:09:26,970
Four: up to date, for strict checks. Strict
means the tested commit must include the

124
00:09:27,010 --> 00:09:31,742
current base branch. git merge-base
--is-ancestor with the base and your HEAD.

125
00:09:31,782 --> 00:09:36,881
If the base isn't an ancestor, you update
the branch, and then you expect the checks

126
00:09:36,921 --> 00:09:39,879
to run again and stale
approvals to be dismissed.

127
00:09:40,219 --> 00:09:45,356
Five: checks. For each required name,
is there a result on the newest commit?

128
00:09:45,396 --> 00:09:50,264
Pending forever means a skipped workflow,
a wrong name, or a wrong event.

129
00:09:50,604 --> 00:09:55,421
Six: reviews. Count approvals from people
with write access given after the last

130
00:09:55,461 --> 00:10:00,217
change of the diff. Look for an outstanding
"changes requested", a missing code

131
00:10:00,257 --> 00:10:05,134
owner, an approval by the last pusher,
unresolved threads, a twin pull request on

132
00:10:05,174 --> 00:10:06,106
the same commit.

133
00:10:06,446 --> 00:10:10,966
Seven: commits. Which commits would land,
and do they satisfy the commit-level

134
00:10:11,006 --> 00:10:15,877
rules: signatures, merge commits under linear
history, and under Enterprise metadata

135
00:10:15,917 --> 00:10:18,274
rules the author and committer addresses.

136
00:10:18,614 --> 00:10:23,444
Eight: method. Is the method you chose
allowed by the ruleset and also enabled in

137
00:10:23,484 --> 00:10:24,887
the repository settings?

138
00:10:25,227 --> 00:10:29,650
Nine: you. Do you have write permission,
and is the pull request still a draft?

139
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:34,565
If everything passes and the button is
still grey, a rule you can't see is the

140
00:10:34,605 --> 00:10:39,475
likely cause: an organization or enterprise
ruleset, or a classic rule visible only

141
00:10:39,515 --> 00:10:43,438
to administrators. Ask an administrator
for the output of step two.

142
00:10:45,278 --> 00:10:49,763
The nine steps as one picture: GitHub's
questions on the left, Git's on the right.

143
00:10:51,603 --> 00:10:56,330
Into the lab. The situation: a repository
called ticket-router on a local bare

144
00:10:56,370 --> 00:11:01,036
server, a repository with no working
tree that plays the server in this demo.

145
00:11:01,076 --> 00:11:05,925
Three branches are open as if they were
pull requests: feature/priority-routing,

146
00:11:05,965 --> 00:11:10,080
which is yours, and fix/threshold
and docs/queues, which are Ravi's.

147
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:13,014
Your local main is one
commit behind the server.

148
00:11:13,354 --> 00:11:16,992
Step 1. Read the tool before
you trust it, then fetch.

149
00:11:17,332 --> 00:11:21,285
git fetch is 🟢 SAFE: it adds objects
and moves remote-tracking refs.

150
00:11:21,325 --> 00:11:25,984
Before the output appears, read the script
aloud. It asks four questions about a

151
00:11:26,024 --> 00:11:30,271
branch, each with one command you know:
how many commits the pull request

152
00:11:30,311 --> 00:11:35,087
introduces, whether origin/main is an
ancestor of the branch, whether a test merge

153
00:11:35,127 --> 00:11:39,198
is clean, and how many merge commits
are among the introduced commits.

154
00:11:39,538 --> 00:11:45,029
Look at the last line of the fetch output.
origin/main, your clone's record of the

155
00:11:45,069 --> 00:11:50,693
server's main, moved from 9a383e5 to 9aa221a.
Every answer from here on is about the

156
00:11:50,733 --> 00:11:53,459
server's main, not about your local main.

157
00:11:53,799 --> 00:11:56,597
Step 2. Run the script for each branch.

158
00:11:56,937 --> 00:12:01,929
Predict first. The main branch requires
linear history, which means no commit with

159
00:12:01,969 --> 00:12:06,716
two parents, and strict, up-to-date
checks. For each branch, which line of the

160
00:12:06,756 --> 00:12:09,723
report would make GitHub
refuse? Say it out loud.

161
00:12:14,563 --> 00:12:19,517
Read it one branch at a time. Your branch:
three commits, a clean test merge, no

162
00:12:19,557 --> 00:12:24,573
merge commits, and "up to date with main:
no". Under a strict status check policy

163
00:12:24,613 --> 00:12:29,629
that one line blocks it. Ravi's fix/threshold:
not up to date, and the test merge

164
00:12:29,669 --> 00:12:34,747
reports a conflict. docs/queues: up to
date, clean, and one merge commit among its

165
00:12:34,787 --> 00:12:39,117
two commits. Under a linear history
rule that's the line that matters.

166
00:12:39,457 --> 00:12:43,542
Three branches, three different answers,
and no browser has been opened.

167
00:12:43,882 --> 00:12:47,184
Step 3. Get the details for
the two that aren't yours.

168
00:12:47,524 --> 00:12:50,659
What will the first command
print besides a tree ID?

169
00:12:50,699 --> 00:12:51,941
Make your prediction.

170
00:12:56,781 --> 00:13:01,440
The first command wrote a tree,
338b4c9, and named the conflicted file:

171
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:06,073
config/routing.yaml. A tree is Git's
object for one directory listing.

172
00:13:06,113 --> 00:13:11,367
Nothing in your working tree or index
changed. merge-tree works on objects only.

173
00:13:11,707 --> 00:13:18,977
The second command shows why docs/queues
has a merge commit: 9042b9e, "Merge main

174
00:13:19,017 --> 00:13:26,016
into docs/queues". Ravi brought the branch
up to date by merging main into it.

175
00:13:26,356 --> 00:13:30,771
That satisfied the up-to-date rule and
broke the linear history rule in the same

176
00:13:30,811 --> 00:13:35,170
step. Leave the question of how docs/queues
could still be merged for the lab's

177
00:13:35,210 --> 00:13:35,783
checkpoint.

178
00:13:36,123 --> 00:13:39,679
The replay continues with a
failure scenario and a recovery.

179
00:13:39,719 --> 00:13:42,736
Stop here. Those belong to
your own run of the lab.

180
00:13:43,076 --> 00:13:47,979
Now the platform side, on your own practice
repository, following Part B of Lab

181
00:13:48,019 --> 00:13:53,235
23.3. The GitHub interface changes. The text
of the lab and the linked documentation

182
00:13:53,275 --> 00:13:57,052
are the reference, and the authors
captured no GitHub output.

183
00:13:57,092 --> 00:14:01,807
Run this part in your normal shell, not
in labs/shell, because the lab shell

184
00:14:01,847 --> 00:14:06,313
switches off the system configuration
where the credential helper lives.

185
00:14:06,653 --> 00:14:11,879
The lab adds a third ruleset that requires
a status check named ci/unit, strict.

186
00:14:11,919 --> 00:14:14,842
Nothing in the repository
reports that check.

187
00:14:15,182 --> 00:14:19,650
This POST is the 🔴 DANGEROUS call whose
five answers you heard in the concept

188
00:14:19,690 --> 00:14:23,748
section. On a practice repository
that only you use, it's appropriate.

189
00:14:24,088 --> 00:14:27,856
Open the address of your practice
repository with /rules added.

190
00:14:27,896 --> 00:14:32,751
You should find the rulesets that are
active, and for each the rules it contains.

191
00:14:32,791 --> 00:14:35,834
Then compare with what gh
ruleset check main lists.

192
00:14:36,174 --> 00:14:39,811
Case A is a pull request whose
required check nobody reports.

193
00:14:39,851 --> 00:14:44,874
Open it in the browser and look at the merge
box. According to the documentation the

194
00:14:44,914 --> 00:14:48,430
box names the rule that blocks
the merge. Then ask the CLI:

195
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:53,786
Name what you see by its function: the
list of required checks GitHub is waiting

196
00:14:53,826 --> 00:14:59,033
for, and the merge state. Case B moves main
while case A is open, so that case A is

197
00:14:59,073 --> 00:15:04,342
no longer up to date. The lab has you confirm
that with git merge-base --is-ancestor

198
00:15:04,382 --> 00:15:09,209
before you touch anything. Case C leaves
no merge method: the ruleset and the

199
00:15:09,249 --> 00:15:14,139
repository settings no longer agree on
one. For each case, open the rules page

200
00:15:14,179 --> 00:15:16,857
first, name the rule,
and only then repair.

201
00:15:18,697 --> 00:15:20,345
Five mistakes to watch for.

202
00:15:20,685 --> 00:15:24,170
Relaxing one ruleset and
expecting the block to go away.

203
00:15:24,210 --> 00:15:29,016
Root cause: a second ruleset, an
organization ruleset, or a classic rule also

204
00:15:29,056 --> 00:15:31,723
applies, and every
applicable rule applies.

205
00:15:32,063 --> 00:15:36,450
Waiting for a check that says
"Waiting for status to be reported".

206
00:15:36,490 --> 00:15:42,085
Root cause: the workflow was skipped by a
path or branch filter or [skip ci], or the

207
00:15:42,125 --> 00:15:46,714
required name is wrong, or the run
used an event that does not count.

208
00:15:47,054 --> 00:15:51,917
Treating an approval as permanent. Root
cause: the approval was dismissed as stale,

209
00:15:51,957 --> 00:15:56,642
or was given by the last pusher, or a code
owner is missing, or another reviewer

210
00:15:56,682 --> 00:15:57,705
requested changes.

211
00:15:58,045 --> 00:16:02,465
Allowing only one merge method in the
ruleset and disabling that method in the

212
00:16:02,505 --> 00:16:07,097
repository settings. Root cause: both
layers must permit the method, and they are

213
00:16:07,137 --> 00:16:08,870
configured in different places.

214
00:16:09,210 --> 00:16:13,939
Trusting a new branch pattern without
testing it. Root cause: * does not cross a

215
00:16:13,979 --> 00:16:18,886
slash, so the pattern matches fewer names
than you meant; gh ruleset check with the

216
00:16:18,926 --> 00:16:20,138
branch name shows it.

217
00:16:21,978 --> 00:16:26,537
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's
worked design is for a service called

218
00:16:26,577 --> 00:16:29,750
inventory-api. It deploys
from main on every merge.

219
00:16:29,790 --> 00:16:34,791
Six engineers, pull requests of a few
hundred lines, and CI, the automated tests

220
00:16:34,831 --> 00:16:39,642
that run on each change, of about eight
minutes. An ML platform team owns the

221
00:16:39,682 --> 00:16:40,902
deployment workflow.

222
00:16:41,242 --> 00:16:45,877
The team has decided on squash merges,
where each pull request lands as one new

223
00:16:45,917 --> 00:16:50,729
commit. The textbook says this is one
defensible design for that context, and that

224
00:16:50,769 --> 00:16:55,404
another team should make other choices.
The ruleset file was assembled from the

225
00:16:55,444 --> 00:16:59,783
documented schema and syntax-checked.
The author didn't send it to GitHub.

226
00:17:00,123 --> 00:17:04,910
Walk through the choices with their costs.
The target is ~DEFAULT_BRANCH, which

227
00:17:04,950 --> 00:17:09,982
survives a rename and has no pattern to get
wrong. The cost is that it protects one

228
00:17:10,022 --> 00:17:14,932
branch only. Deletions and force pushes
are blocked, because main is deployed and

229
00:17:14,972 --> 00:17:20,004
its history is an audit record. The cost
is that a bad merge is undone by a revert,

230
00:17:20,044 --> 00:17:21,042
never by a reset.

231
00:17:21,382 --> 00:17:25,644
Linear history with squash only gives
one commit per pull request and trivial

232
00:17:25,684 --> 00:17:29,890
reverts. The cost is that the reviewed
commit IDs aren't the commit on main.

233
00:17:30,230 --> 00:17:34,498
One approval with stale approvals
dismissed means nothing lands unseen.

234
00:17:34,538 --> 00:17:39,169
The cost is re-approval after each update.
Approval of the last push covers a

235
00:17:39,209 --> 00:17:43,901
reviewer who pushes a fix and approves
it. Code owner review is on because the

236
00:17:43,941 --> 00:17:48,937
deployment workflow has named owners. Owners
become a bottleneck if the file is too

237
00:17:48,977 --> 00:17:49,301
broad.

238
00:17:49,641 --> 00:17:54,100
One required check, strict, means the
tested commit includes current main.

239
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:58,720
With eight-minute CI and a few merges
a day that costs some waiting, and the

240
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:03,280
textbook names a merge queue as the
alternative when updates start to race.

241
00:18:03,320 --> 00:18:08,265
The bypass list is empty, so administrators
follow the same path, and an emergency

242
00:18:08,305 --> 00:18:13,129
needs a deliberate, recorded edit of the
ruleset. There's no signed-commit rule,

243
00:18:13,169 --> 00:18:18,175
because squash commits are signed by GitHub
anyway and requiring signatures on head

244
00:18:18,215 --> 00:18:21,032
branches would block
contributors without keys.

245
00:18:21,372 --> 00:18:25,978
Then the two things this ruleset doesn't
do. It doesn't protect release tags.

246
00:18:26,018 --> 00:18:30,866
That's a tag ruleset. And it doesn't stop
a required check from being reported by

247
00:18:30,906 --> 00:18:35,391
anyone with write access. For that you
pin the check to its GitHub App with

248
00:18:35,431 --> 00:18:36,296
integration_id.

249
00:18:36,636 --> 00:18:42,012
Rolling it out: create it Disabled, run gh
ruleset check main and read the result,

250
00:18:42,052 --> 00:18:45,183
open a test pull request,
then switch to Active.

251
00:18:47,023 --> 00:18:51,600
Your turn. Do Lab 23.3, "Three
blocked merges to diagnose", in

252
00:18:51,640 --> 00:18:55,770
lab-manual/m23-governance.md.
Part A runs in labs/shell.

253
00:18:55,810 --> 00:19:01,653
The clock is real there, so your commit
IDs differ from the ones in this video.

254
00:19:01,693 --> 00:19:06,196
Part B runs in your normal shell
on your practice repository.

255
00:19:06,536 --> 00:19:11,121
Before you run anything in Part A, write
down for each of the three branches which

256
00:19:11,161 --> 00:19:15,747
rule of Chapter 18 would refuse it. Before
each repair in Part B, write down which

257
00:19:15,787 --> 00:19:19,470
rule blocks, and what GitHub will
require again after your repair.

258
00:19:19,510 --> 00:19:22,177
Then check your prediction
against what you see.

259
00:19:22,517 --> 00:19:28,157
The challenge is Exercise
23.7, "Protect a monorepo", in

260
00:19:28,197 --> 00:19:30,997
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

261
00:19:34,837 --> 00:19:39,762
Answer out loud first. A strong answer has
an order, and says why the order is that

262
00:19:39,802 --> 00:19:43,709
way. It starts with evidence from
the platform, not with a repair.

263
00:19:43,749 --> 00:19:48,614
It names every layer where a rule can live,
including the ones the developer can't

264
00:19:48,654 --> 00:19:53,340
see. For each rule it names the Git fact
the rule inspects and the command that

265
00:19:53,380 --> 00:19:54,955
computes that fact locally.

266
00:19:55,295 --> 00:19:59,369
It mentions what an update of the
branch costs under strict checks and

267
00:19:59,409 --> 00:20:04,189
stale-approval dismissal. And it's ready
for the follow-up, the mirror case: how a

268
00:20:04,229 --> 00:20:07,363
colleague was able to merge
without meeting the rules.

269
00:20:07,403 --> 00:20:10,537
Say which layer acts in every
sentence: Git or GitHub.

270
00:20:12,377 --> 00:20:17,495
Let's land this. The grey button from the
opening has its answer: read the merge

271
00:20:17,535 --> 00:20:22,266
box, list every rule from every layer,
then ask Git one question per rule.

272
00:20:22,606 --> 00:20:24,266
You should now be able to say:

273
00:20:24,606 --> 00:20:29,772
Anyone with read access can see the active
rulesets on the /rules page; classic

274
00:20:29,812 --> 00:20:34,978
rules are visible only to administrators,
and disabled rulesets are not on that

275
00:20:35,018 --> 00:20:35,307
page.

276
00:20:35,647 --> 00:20:40,831
gh ruleset only reads; rulesets are created
and changed through the REST API, and

277
00:20:40,871 --> 00:20:44,442
every such call that is not
a GET is labelled dangerous.

278
00:20:44,782 --> 00:20:50,081
A blocked merge is diagnosed from the
outside in: the merge box, every rule from

279
00:20:50,121 --> 00:20:53,017
every layer, then one
Git question per rule.

280
00:20:53,357 --> 00:20:56,217
BLOCKED says that a rule is
unmet, and not which one.

281
00:20:56,557 --> 00:21:00,231
Every rule in a production ruleset
has a cost, and you can name it.

282
00:21:02,071 --> 00:21:09,299
Read sections 18.16 to 18.21 of Chapter
18 and do the Practice section 18.23.

283
00:21:09,639 --> 00:21:14,628
Today you turned "why can't I merge" from
three guesses into one procedure, and you

284
00:21:14,668 --> 00:21:17,899
can name what each rule of
a production ruleset costs.

285
00:21:17,939 --> 00:21:22,988
Practise the four preflight questions on a
branch of your own before the next video.

286
00:21:23,028 --> 00:21:27,773
Next time: CODEOWNERS, what it is, where
it lives, its syntax, and why the last

287
00:21:27,813 --> 00:21:31,650
match wins. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

288
00:21:31,690 --> 00:21:33,104
See you in the next one.
